# Rustty-only Valgrind suppressions for G-VALGRIND (check_valgrind.sh), kept
# apart from `valgrind.supp`, which stays the reference's file verbatim.
#
# Scope: Mesa's llvmpipe, the software OpenGL driver the renderer's live-GL
# tests run on (LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1, RUSTTY_REQUIRE_GL=1). The reference's
# `valgrind.supp` hides graphics-driver noise the same way, by naming the
# driver's own frames (its "Mesa", "Graphics Driver dri", "i965 addr4/addr8" and
# "mesa memcpy/memcmp" entries). The reference runs no test that draws, so it
# has no llvmpipe entries; these are the port's, one group per finding
# (NEW-FINDINGS N412), each measured on the Fedora 44 aarch64 VM (Mesa 26.1.4,
# LLVM 22.1.8, valgrind 3.27.1).

# llvmpipe compiles its shaders with LLVM (gallivm) at draw time; memcheck
# reports conditional jumps inside LLVM's code generator (AsmPrinter,
# SelectionDAG lowering) on a value created by a stack allocation in Mesa's
# `lp_build_create_jit_compiler_for_module`. Reproduced by
# `.port/tools/valgrind-llvmpipe-probe/probe.c` (C, EGL surfaceless, no Rustty
# code at all), and by a single-triangle variant of it, so it is
# driver-internal. Every frame between the report and
# `gallivm_jit_function` belongs to LLVM or Mesa; a Rustty defect cannot be the
# top of such a stack.
{
   llvmpipe-gallivm-llvm-codegen
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:gallivm_jit_function
}

# The golden tests read back pixels llvmpipe rasterized and branch on them.
# Memcheck marks some of those pixels undefined, with the origin inside llvmpipe
# (`lp_scene_new_data_block`, the scene memory its JIT-compiled rasterizer
# writes), never in a buffer Rustty uploaded. The C program
# `.port/tools/valgrind-llvmpipe-probe/probe.c`, drawing 64 instanced quads
# from fully written buffers with the renderer's premultiplied-alpha blend, gets
# the same report on its own pixel comparisons (origin: a stack slot of the
# JIT-compiled code) while every one of its 4,096 pixels has the expected
# value, and none with blending off: blending scales the destination by
# (1 - source alpha) = 0, and memcheck does not know a product with a defined
# zero is defined. The values these tests assert on are right too (they pass).
# `--expensive-definedness-checks=yes` does not remove the reports. These
# entries match only a conditional jump whose
# TOP frames are the test's own pixel comparisons (the test body, its
# `Iterator::any` predicates, the slice `!=`, `Ord::max`), so a Rustty function
# the test calls is still checked in full.
{
   golden-readback-cursor-fg-bg-body
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_ZN15rustty_renderer7generic10linux_impl5tests27golden_renders_cursor_fg_bg17h*E
}
{
   golden-readback-cursor-fg-bg-max
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_ZN4core3cmp3Ord3max17h*E
   fun:_ZN15rustty_renderer7generic10linux_impl5tests27golden_renders_cursor_fg_bg17h*E
}
{
   golden-readback-custom-shader-passthrough-body
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_ZN15rustty_renderer7generic10linux_impl5tests40golden_renders_custom_shader_passthrough*
}
{
   golden-readback-custom-shader-passthrough-any
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_ZN4core4iter6traits8iterator8Iterator3any5check*
   fun:_ZN4core4iter6traits8iterator8Iterator8try_fold17h*E
   fun:_ZN4core4iter6traits8iterator8Iterator3any17h*E
   fun:_ZN15rustty_renderer7generic10linux_impl5tests40golden_renders_custom_shader_passthrough17h*E
}
{
   golden-readback-custom-shader-passthrough-slice-ne
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:bcmp
   fun:*SlicePartialEq*equal*
   fun:not_equal<*
   fun:*PartialEq*ne*
   fun:*PartialEq*ne*
   fun:_ZN15rustty_renderer7generic10linux_impl5tests40golden_renders_custom_shader_passthrough28_$u7b$$u7b$closure*
}
